## The A_FMT_emit row and the header (2026-10-31)

The copy of record before this change (sha256 27619ca3af9e7f3ccbf17fd1f285b98a4ba38ae645991840be8d2f17e384de55) wrote
A_exact row 39 `KV 0x102a8 0x214c0;   (* CONTENT: unique in asan .rodata on 32 bytes *)` (file blob 4dec0001329f7e9118840ba37dbaba853b5f2da5,
sha256 8767cfd8a008e6b925ebb9481ab3bb97c3eab5bde7d046d1e88c754787cbd11f). Donor 0x102a8 is the printf model's A_FMT_emit
(lib/CJ/RL/GX/Printf_model.v:475), whose string FMT_emit is "%s\n\0" (:466). IImgP carries no object at 0x102a8: it is
the END of IImgP's interval [0x1029e,0x102a8)+88, and IImgP's intervals are half-open (IInst.v:15, `k <? hi`). That
generator carried the key to the bound 0x102a8 + 88 = 0x10300 and marked the row a bound, which its CODEREF pass skips
but its CONTENT pass did not: it paired the bound with the 32 bytes that start at plain 0x10300
("<format | *> <cpu-list|-> <nice|"), which occur once in the fuzz_command.asan .rodata, at 0x214c0. Those bytes are not
"%s\n\0". No lemma of RT_IImgA_ok.v read the row, so the wrong pairing compiled, although the header said that every row
is checked again by RT_IImgA_ok.v.

The three changes: the CONTENT pass skips a bound row as the CODEREF pass does (`or r.get("bound")` in its skip test);
the comment over the OPEN rows says what IImgP does with each key instead of calling every one of them a bound; and the
header says that RT_IImgA_ok.v checks the rows its lemmas name and that its header lists them, instead of saying that
every row is checked. The regenerated file differs from the earlier one in line 2, in that header sentence, in row 39
(removed), and in the OPEN comment, under which the generator now writes
`(* OPEN donor 0x102a8 (3 uses): interval-end of [0x1029e,0x102a8)+88 *)`. Every other row is byte-identical. No program
code of fuzz_command.asan addresses any copy of "%s\n\0" (its one standalone copy is the sanitizer runtime's), and no proof
reads the bytes at sym 0x102a8, so the key keeps no row.

